     "result_text_tail": "te.\n  - Page 7 shows totals and the gap to K as two column charts; the line-item build-up is on backup page 20.\n  - Capacity and one-time cost are drawn against dashed capacity and authority lines.\n  - Page 13's lease table became sidebar bullets with the same content.\n- **Analysis assumptions kept, not checked against the pack:** these four are kept as written and labelled as assumptions or proposals on the slides:\n  - the $0.25M cap on the cross-dock test cost\n  - the 90% peak-week gate\n  - the gate requiring the cross-dock to cost at least $0.2M a year less than K\n  - the 95% guardrail for East\n- **Number formatting:** outside charts, the renderer would round $M values to one decimal (the $0.75M exit fee would show as $0.8M). Those values are written as exact text instead, each backed by a data point. After rendering, a script I wrote (`work/augment_workbook.py`) adds them to each page's sheet; the stock renderer can't list them there.\n- **Navigation and owners:**\n  - No section dividers, since the deck has 15 or fewer content pages.\n  - Next steps name the Board only where the source does. The two management actions have no owner, because neither file names one.\n\nThe spec and build scripts are in `./work/`. To re-render, run `render.py` on `work/northline-east-dc.json`, then run `work/augment_workbook.py` (it needs the spec and workbook paths) to restore the workbook rows. I can switch the theme (crimson, forest, mono, vivid, slate, terracotta) if you prefer another look."
